{"claim":{"id":"1dcf2bdd-dd23-413c-96a7-b5d695bb0a31","canonical_form":"Stable microscopic black holes produced at the LHC would endanger Earth.","claim_type":"empirical_derived","state":"active","decomposition_status":"complete","importance":0.35,"tags":[]},"assessment":{"status":"contradicted","confidence":0.85,"claim_credence":0.01,"summary":"The proposition is the danger thesis that anchored public concern about the Large Hadron Collider before its 2008 start, and the examined evidence weighs against it on each of its legs. The claim is an extreme conditional compound: it requires that the LHC produce black holes at all, which presupposes speculative physics in which [[claim:e382b679-7f22-451c-a551-864fc0c798b5|large extra dimensions lower the Planck scale to the TeV range]], a scenario collider and astrophysical constraints have increasingly disfavored and dedicated searches have never confirmed; it requires stability against evaporation, contrary to the strong theoretical expectation that [[claim:fcab83dc-9428-40e2-86dc-457774afaafa|any collider-produced black hole evaporates essentially instantly via Hawking radiation]]; and it requires that [[claim:6a7966e4-5704-4295-adbf-837de96f26cd|a stable trapped black hole accrete matter fast enough to grow and destroy the planet]], the step on which the direct evidence turns.

The peer-reviewed analysis of Giddings and Mangano (https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3381, Physical Review D 78, 035009 (2008)) grants production and stability for the sake of argument and finds no surviving danger: scenarios in which a trapped black hole could grow dangerously fast are excluded because cosmic rays striking far denser white dwarfs and neutron stars would have produced the same black holes and destroyed those long-lived, well-observed bodies long ago. The observational premise is solid: [[claim:11887800-2f7b-4ac5-99c2-4dc5bb2f7f7a|cosmic rays above LHC energies have struck Earth, the Moon, and dense stars for billions of years]]. Independent work reaches the same verdict, including the CERN-affiliated review by Ellis, Giudice, Mangano, Tkachev, and Wiedemann (https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3414) and the Casadio, Fabi, and Harms analysis in the warped brane-world framework (https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2948). A decade and a half of LHC operation, including dedicated CMS and ATLAS searches with no microscopic black hole events, weighs further against production at any reachable scale.

The one credible contrary voice is Plaga's preprint (https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1415), which constructs a metastable scenario in which black holes accrete at the Eddington limit and evade the astrophysical exclusions; it concedes that the mainstream analyses are technically correct under their assumptions and attributes the different verdicts to different initial assumptions. Giddings and Mangano's published reply (https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.4087) argues the scenario is internally inconsistent, the claimed black hole radius exceeding the Schwarzschild radius for the relevant mass range by many orders of magnitude, and that the white dwarf and neutron star bounds would apply regardless. The literature has not taken the scenario up. For the claim to revive, a derivation would need to show dangerous accretion rates for a stable trapped black hole in a parameter range that survives the compact-star constraints; no published argument presently does.","reasoning_trace":"Instance distribution: five sources deny the claim (Giddings-Mangano PRD 78, 035009 (2008), read in full from stored text; Giddings-Mangano's reply arXiv:0808.4087; the Ellis et al. review arXiv:0806.3414; Casadio, Fabi, and Harms arXiv:0901.2948; plus the null CMS/ATLAS searches, which deny the production premise rather than the danger thesis itself) and one affirms it (Plaga arXiv:0808.1415, recorded at reduced confidence 0.75 because its scenario turns on metastability and harm from Hawking radiation emitted during Eddington-limited accretion, so it is a partial, framework-dependent assertion of the canonical claim rather than a direct one). The dissent is a preprint whose full derivation was not readable this pass; its abstract concedes the mainstream technical conclusions are correct under their assumptions. The reply's internal-inconsistency argument and the independent Casadio et al. conclusion in the same warped brane-world framework weigh against taking the dissent as live. Adversarial pass: before agreeing with the mature consensus I looked for an error in doing so, specifically whether the white dwarf and neutron star exclusion (d7357393) transfers across all parameter space and whether the accretion-rate crux (6a7966e4) could hold in ranges the analyses omit. Plaga's evasion claim is exactly that challenge; the reply answers it with an inconsistency in the scenario's own radius-to-mass relation and with the applicability of the compact-star bounds even granting the scenario. LHC search limits (semiclassical black hole masses excluded up to 9 to 11.4 TeV in large-extra-dimension models per recent CMS analyses) bear on the production conditional (e382b679) without refuting its internal logic. What would change the verdict: a peer-reviewed derivation of rapid dangerous accretion for a stable trapped black hole that survives the reply's objections and the compact-star constraints, or evidence that the astrophysical exclusion argument (dab2fd70, the robustness claim, currently unassessed in the graph) fails in a way no reply covers. Sources read closely: only the Giddings-Mangano analysis in stored full text; the others through abstracts and search excerpts, which is proportionate to the claim's importance; the preprint-versus-reply exchange is the one place a deeper pass would go.","assessed_at":"2026-09-18T17:52:19.105Z","model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash"},"formalization":null,"formalization_pending":null,"formalization_history":[],"verification":null,"lean_checks":[],"lean_checks_total":0,"subclaim_count":6,"arguments":[{"id":"3f213d80-ff86-43a9-a172-571b29afc1b0","name":"Danger case: production, stability, and rapid accretion","stance":"for","content":"Because [[claim:e382b679-7f22-451c-a551-864fc0c798b5|the LHC could produce microscopic black holes if large extra dimensions lower the Planck scale to the TeV range]], and because [[claim:6a7966e4-5704-4295-adbf-837de96f26cd|a stable black hole trapped on Earth would accrete matter fast enough to grow and destroy the planet]], a produced black hole that failed to evaporate would endanger Earth. The first premise grants production under speculative extra-dimension physics; the second, granting stability, does the danger work: rapid accretion is the mechanism by which a stable microscopic black hole becomes catastrophic rather than an inert curiosity sinking to Earth's center.","evidence_urls":[]},{"id":"b779f648-a83f-4b45-8d63-7bab5aef968a","name":"Safety case: cosmic-ray record and astrophysical exclusions","stance":"against","content":"The danger thesis fails in the mainstream scenario because [[claim:fcab83dc-9428-40e2-86dc-457774afaafa|any collider-produced black hole evaporates essentially instantly via Hawking radiation]], and it fails even granting stability, because [[claim:11887800-2f7b-4ac5-99c2-4dc5bb2f7f7a|cosmic rays above LHC energies have struck Earth, the Moon, and dense stars for billions of years]] without harm: the dense stellar record underwrites [[claim:d7357393-c216-45e1-92bc-04896a5af2ad|the exclusion of dangerous accretion by observed white dwarfs and neutron stars]], and the accretion analyses carry that exclusion over to [[claim:dab2fd70-94b8-462a-bbb6-2d3221b5b351|Earth even without Hawking radiation]].","evidence_urls":[]}]}
